---
import Layout from "@components/Layout.astro";
import PageHeader from "@components/PageHeader.astro";
import CardGrid from "@components/CardGrid.astro";
import { Card, Alert } from "webcoreui/astro";
import CardAnalysis from "@components/CardAnalysis.astro";
---

<Layout
    title="Maindeck Analysis - Replenish Guide"
    description="Deep dive into the core cards and strategy of the Replenish deck. Understand the purpose of every enchantment, cantrip, and interaction spell in the maindeck and sideboard."
>
    <PageHeader title="Maindeck Analysis" />
    <Card title="The Core" titleTag="h2">
        <CardGrid
            width="180px"
            cards={[
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/sorceries/replenish.jpg",
                    alt: "Replenish",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/enchantments/opalescence.jpg",
                    alt: "Opalescence",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/enchantments/parallax_wave.webp",
                    alt: "Parallax Wave",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/enchantments/attunement.jpg",
                    alt: "Attunement",
                },
            ]}
        />

        <Card secondary={true} style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
            <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem;">
                <div>
                    <h3
                        style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                    >
                        Replenish (4 main)
                    </h3>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        The non-negotiable cornerstone of the archetype.
                        Delivering massive, asymmetric tempo and permanent
                        swings, running four copies is strictly essential
                        regardless of your configuration.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3
                        style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                    >
                        Opalescence (4 main)
                    </h3>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        Transforms all non-Aura enchantments into creatures with
                        power and toughness equal to their mana value. Note that
                        Opalescence does not animate itself—a second copy on the
                        battlefield is required to animate the first into a 4/4.
                        Under a single Opalescence, your primary threats become
                        massive combat clocks: Decree of Silence (8/8), Parallax
                        Wave (4/4), Attunement (3/3), and Seal of Cleansing
                        (2/2).
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3
                        style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                    >
                        Parallax Wave (4 main)
                    </h3>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        Serves a dual role as early tactical defense and lethal
                        end-game engine. Defensively, it controls hostile
                        creature boards; paired with Opalescence, it initiates a
                        permanent, one-sided creature exile loop while
                        presenting a 4/4 body.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3
                        style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                    >
                        Attunement (4 main)
                    </h3>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        The foundational selection engine that digs three cards
                        deep, curates the graveyard for Replenish, and attacks
                        as a 3/3 under Opalescence. Avoid blind activations.
                        Treat Attunement as a precise tactical tool rather than
                        casual draw; before activating, explicitly identify the
                        cards you intend to discard. If your current hand
                        structure already dictates your upcoming sequence and
                        lacks redundant discard fodder, preserve your hand and
                        hold the activation.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Card>
    </Card>

    <Card title="The Lock Piece" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Decree of Silence (2-3 main)"
            image="enchantments/decree_of_silence.jpg"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Replacing Parallax Tide as the premier lock piece, Decree has
                proven exceptional across multiple tactical axes. While
                assembling the complete Opal-Wave-Decree combo guarantees
                immediate victory, simply discarding Decree to Attunement or
                Frantic Search and returning it via Replenish forces opponents
                to expend massive resources fighting through three consecutive
                countered spells.
            </p>
            <p>
                In control matchups, cycling Decree for six mana serves as an
                uncounterable cantrip that dismantles key countermagic or
                game-winning threats while maintaining card flow. Opponents
                frequently pass it off an Intuition pile assuming an eight-mana
                enchantment is inert, only to lose immense tempo to its cycling
                trigger. Furthermore, under Opalescence, it hits the battlefield
                as a commanding 8/8 finisher.
            </p>
            <p>
                Run the third copy when anticipating a diverse or combo-heavy
                metagame. Against dedicated control shells—where velocity
                engines like Frantic Search and Intuition are trimmed
                post-sideboard—dropping to two copies minimizes clunky draws
                when discard outlets are scarce.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Proactive Disruption" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Meddling Mage (0-4 main)"
            image="creatures/meddling_mage.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Running four copies across the 75 is tournament standard;
                positioning them in the maindeck is a proactive metagame choice.
                Maindeck Mages excel in structured paper environments with
                shared game clocks, offering immediate interference against fast
                combo, Sligh, Stiflenought, and Landstill.
            </p>
            <p>
                Naming conventions demand strict situational awareness. Against
                Sligh, naming Lightning Bolt is designed primarily to preserve
                Mage as a stable blocker against Jackal Pup, later pivoting to
                closing burn spells like Fireblast or Ball Lightning. Against
                control, prioritize Counterspell or Swords to Plowshares.
                Against Stiflenought, lock out Phyrexian Dreadnought or Stifle.
                Against Enchantress, name Argothian Enchantress or Enchantress's
                Presence in Game 1, shifting to Tormod's Crypt in Game 2.
            </p>
            <p>
                Mage underperforms against green heavy hitters, midrange builds,
                and discard suites (e.g., The Rock, Ponza, Terrageddon), where
                opposing creatures outclass a 2/2 body and spells are less
                central to victory. In fields heavily populated by these
                archetypes, transition Mages to the sideboard.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Card Selection" titleTag="h2">
        <CardGrid
            width="180px"
            cards={[
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/instants/frantic_search.webp",
                    alt: "Frantic Search",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/instants/intuition.webp",
                    alt: "Intuition",
                },
            ]}
        />

        <Card secondary={true} style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
            <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem;">
                <div>
                    <h3
                        style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                    >
                        Frantic Search (2-3 main)
                    </h3>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        Provides essential deck velocity and enables explosive
                        development turns when paired with sol lands or
                        painlands. However, because it incurs intrinsic card
                        disadvantage, it is the most frequently trimmed tool
                        post-sideboard against grinding control strategies where
                        resource conservation is critical.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3
                        style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                    >
                        Intuition (3-4 main)
                    </h3>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        The archetype's most versatile selection engine and
                        skill-testing tool. The standard combo pile is
                        Opalescence, Parallax Wave, and Decree of
                        Silence—guaranteeing that whichever piece the opponent
                        selects, the other two fuel a subsequent Replenish. Yet
                        piles must adapt dynamically to the board state: against
                        Landstill, searching for three foundational lands
                        secures crucial early drops; against fast aggro,
                        tutoring for three copies of Swords to Plowshares
                        ensures survival. Trimming copies against reactive blue
                        decks is common practice to preserve overall threat
                        density.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Card>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Interaction" titleTag="h2">
        <CardGrid
            width="180px"
            cards={[
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/instants/swords_to_plowshares.webp",
                    alt: "Swords to Plowshares",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/enchantments/seal_of_cleansing.webp",
                    alt: "Seal of Cleansing",
                },
                {
                    src: "/src/assets/cards/artifacts/tsabos_web.jpg",
                    alt: "Tsabo’s Web",
                },
            ]}
        />

        <Card secondary={true} style="margin-bottom: 3rem;">
            <div style="display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1.5rem;">
                <div>
                    <h3
                        style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                    >
                        Swords to Plowshares (3-4 main, 0-1 side)
                    </h3>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        The gold standard of one-mana spot removal. Three copies
                        in the maindeck keep the strategy efficient and
                        streamlined, while bringing in the fourth post-sideboard
                        solidifies your defenses against creature-heavy builds.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3
                        style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                    >
                        Seal of Cleansing (2-3 main, 1 side)
                    </h3>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        Provides proactive interaction against problematic
                        enchantments and mana artifacts. Because it can be
                        sacrificed for instant tactical value and reanimated via
                        Replenish, it functions as a recurring control engine.
                        Easily sideboarded out against opponents lacking
                        targets.
                    </p>
                </div>
                <div>
                    <h3
                        style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.5rem; color: var(--w-color-primary-10);"
                    >
                        Tsabo’s Web (0-3 main, 1-3 side)
                    </h3>
                    <p style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                        A clean, disruptive answer to utility lands like
                        Rishadan Port, Dust Bowl, and Mishra's Factory. The
                        precise number across the 75 shifts between one and
                        three depending on the prevalence of mana-denial
                        structures in your local metagame.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </Card>
    </Card>

    <Card title="No Maindeck Mana Leak" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Mana Leak (0-3 main)"
            image="instants/mana_leak.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Maindecking Mana Leak is an active metagame calculation. In
                environments dominated by patient control strategies like
                Landstill—where games extend and opponents accumulate ample mana
                to pay the additional three—it suffers noticeable diminishing
                returns. However, as faster interactive decks like Esper
                Psychatog and post-ban Replenish mirrors gain prominence, cheap
                interactive countermagic rises in value. If your local field
                demands robust early-turn protection, Mana Leak provides an
                exceptionally strong early shield.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="Other Cards Worth Considering" titleTag="h2">
        <CardAnalysis
            title="Deep Analysis"
            image="sorceries/deep_analysis.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                While omitted from stock builds, Deep Analysis transforms
                Intuition into a potent card advantage generator in slower
                matchups where card volume matters more than raw velocity.
                Rather than tutoring for combo pieces against heavy
                control—which risks exposing threats to graveyard hate—tutoring
                for Deep Analysis ensures persistent draw power from both hand
                and graveyard. In metagames saturated with Landstill or
                comparable slow blue decks, one maindeck copy deserves serious
                consideration.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Fact or Fiction"
            image="instants/fact_or_fiction.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                A defining staple for reactive, instant-speed variations of the
                deck that actively leverage countermagic suites like Mana Leak
                and Counterspell. While the four-mana investment is non-trivial,
                digging five cards deep provides premier selection while
                generating skill-testing piles that put tremendous cognitive
                pressure on your opponent.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>
    </Card>

    <Card title="The Mana Base" titleTag="h2">
        <h3 style="margin-top: 0;">Mana Base Theory</h3>
        <p>
            <strong>25-26 Lands</strong><br />Extensive testing across 24, 25,
            and 26 lands reveals twenty-five as the optimal threshold for
            balancing reliable early land drops with spell density, though
            twenty-six remains entirely justifiable in mana-hungry
            configurations.
        </p>
        <div
            style="display: flex; gap: 1.5rem; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; align-items: flex-start; flex-wrap: wrap;"
        >
            <div style="flex-shrink: 0; max-width: 100%;">
                <CardGrid
                    cards={[
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/lands/island.jpg",
                            alt: "Island",
                        },
                        {
                            src: "/src/assets/cards/lands/plains.jpg",
                            alt: "Plains",
                        },
                    ]}
                    width="220px"
                />
            </div>
            <div style="flex: 1; min-width: 300px;">
                <h3 style="margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0.75rem;">
                    Ten Basics (5 Island, 5 Plains)
                </h3>
                <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                    Running five basic Islands and five basic Plains insulates
                    the strategy against aggressive non-basic punishment like
                    Wasteland and Price of Progress. Securing your first three
                    land drops as basics is a crucial stabilizing line against
                    burn and mana-denial builds. Furthermore, this distribution
                    strictly adheres to Karsten's mana consistency thresholds,
                    providing ~16 sources of each color to cast double-pipped
                    four-drops on schedule.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="Skycloud Expanse (3-4 main)"
            image="lands/skycloud_expanse.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Trimming from four down to three copies accommodates the fifth
                basic Island and Plains, intentionally prioritizing resilience
                against non-basic hate over redundant mana filtering.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <CardAnalysis
            title="City of Traitors (0)"
            image="lands/city_of_traitors.webp"
        >
            <p style="margin-top: 0;">
                Without Parallax Tide to rapidly truncate games, post-ban
                Replenish relies heavily on mid-to-late game tactical stamina.
                City of Traitors imposes severe sequencing constraints that
                actively degrade your board presence in prolonged attrition
                battles.
            </p>
        </CardAnalysis>

        <div style="margin-bottom: 0.5rem; margin-top: 1rem;">
            <Alert theme="warning">
                <strong>Turn-One Sequencing</strong><br />When evaluating your
                opening plays, leading with a dual land is generally your best
                move to maximize color flexibility for whatever you draw on turn
                two. However, if you suspect your opponent is running Wasteland,
                leading with a basic land is the safer path to avoid losing
                tempo.
            </Alert>
        </div>
    </Card>
</Layout>
